issue.body:
I suggested to our Chief-Architect to move from Markdown to AsciiDoc, since AsciiDoc is mightier and doesn't need crutches, like Markdown, in the form of an endless list of different incompatible Language-"Flavors".
But he disagreed, since the AsciiDoc plugin misses a killer feature for him I also use heavily: To execute code directly from the instruction manuals. I explained to him that AsciiDoc can do this too, but only from the editor, not additionally from the rendering, like Markdown can.

Since he wasn't really convinced, and I also really miss to execute source code directly from the rendering, I added it. I also added scripting language support for JavaScript, PowerShell, Python and Ruby. Since I know from personal experience that bash, PowerShell and Python are constantly used for project setup and management. To break out of my bubble, I researched what other projects use, and added JavaScript and Ruby, since they seem to be extremely popular with project setups.

Yes, JavaScript, it runs with `node` and looks like this:
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const sourceFile = 'blocks_after.adoc';
const destinationFile = 'tmp.txt';
fs.copyFile(sourceFile, destinationFile, (err) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error('Error copying file:', err);
  } else {
    console.log('File copied successfully!');
  }
});
```

I also added a copy button, so the user can tweak the code before running it in his/her favorite IDE.

I added this PR as a draft, since these are my first steps in this plugin, and I'm really insecure if this is OK.

